(movie_id, user_id) is the primary key (column with unique values) for this table.

This table contains the rating of a movie by a user in their review.

created_at is the user's review date. 
 

Write a solution to:

• Find the name of the user who has rated the greatest number of movies. In case of a tie, return the lexicographically smaller user name.

• Find the movie name with the highest average rating in February 2020. In case of a tie, return the lexicographically smaller movie name.


# =================================================== #
                      SOLUTION
# =================================================== #


import pandas as pd

def movie_rating(movies: pd.DataFrame, users: pd.DataFrame, movie_rating: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:
    
    most_rating = (
        movie_rating['user_id']
        .value_counts()
        .reset_index(name = 'total_ratings')
    )

    top_user_rating = (
        pd.merge(
            left = users,
            right = most_rating,
            how = 'left',
            on = 'user_id'
            )
            .drop(columns = ['user_id'])
            .sort_values(by = ['total_ratings', 'name'], ascending = [False, True]).head(1)
        )['name'].iloc[0]

    time_mask = movie_rating['created_at'].between('2020-02-01', '2020-02-28', inclusive = 'both')

    average_rating = (
        movie_rating.loc[time_mask][['movie_id', 'rating']]
        .groupby(by = 'movie_id')['rating']
        .mean()
        .reset_index(name = 'average_ratings')
    )
    
    top_movie_rating = (
        pd.merge(
            left = movies,
            right = average_rating,
            how = 'inner',
            on = 'movie_id'
        )
        .drop(columns = ['movie_id'])
        .sort_values(by = ['average_ratings', 'title'], ascending = [False, True]).head(1)
    )['title'].iloc[0]

    result = pd.DataFrame(data = [top_user_rating, top_movie_rating], columns = ['results'])
    
    return result
